---
layout: article
title: Email triggers
description: Execute Appwrite Functions when your app receives email. Use the built-in address of each function or connect your own domain.
difficulty: beginner
readtime: 6
---

Appwrite Functions can execute when an email arrives at an address that is connected to the function.
Appwrite receives the email, stores its content and attachments, and executes your function in the background with the parsed email as the request body.
Your function can save the email to a table, reply to the sender with Messaging, or send the content to another service.

Email triggers only receive email. To send email from your app, use [Messaging](/docs/products/messaging).

# How email triggers work {% #how-email-triggers-work %}

- Each email that arrives at a connected address creates one [asynchronous execution](/docs/products/functions/execute#asynchronous-executions).
- The execution is a `POST` request to the `/` path of your function, and the `x-appwrite-trigger` header is `email`.
- When one email is sent to more than one address of the same function, the function executes one time and receives all of these addresses.
- Appwrite accepts the email before your function executes. A failed execution does not send an error to the sender.
- Appwrite rejects email that is sent to a function without an active deployment.

# Built-in address {% #built-in-address %}

{% info title="SMTP-DOCS-TODO: Screenshot pending (built-in-address)" %}
**SMTP-DOCS-TODO screenshot:built-in-address.** Capture the **Domains** tab of a function that shows the built-in email address card. Light and dark, 1460x822 viewport at 2x, screenshot mode on. Save as `/images/docs/functions/email/built-in-address.avif` and `/images/docs/functions/email/dark/built-in-address.avif`, then replace this block with `only_light` and `only_dark` image tags.
{% /info %}

Each function has a built-in email address that ends with `<FUNCTION_ID>.appwrite.email`.
You can use any name before the `@`. For example, `support@<FUNCTION_ID>.appwrite.email` and `invoices@<FUNCTION_ID>.appwrite.email` both execute the same function, and the function can read which address received the email.
You do not need to configure DNS records for the built-in address.

